<h1>breadth-first search</h1>
<p>
(algorithm)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
Any search algorithm that considers neighbors of a <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertex</em></a>, that is, outgoing <a href="edge.html"><em>edges</em></a> of the vertex's predecessor in the search, before any outgoing edges of the vertex.  Extremes are searched last.  This is typically implemented with a <a href="queue.html"><em>queue</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Aggregate child</strong> (... is a part of or used in me.)<br>
<a href="queue.html"><em>queue</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="depthfirst.html"><em>depth-first search</em></a>, <a href="bestfirst.html"><em>best-first search</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
In a tree, equivalent to a <a href="levelOrderTraversal.html"><em>level-order traversal</em></a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
Lecture notes from Design and Analysis of Algorithms on <a href="http://www.ics.uci.edu/~eppstein/161/960215.html" target="_blank">Breadth-first search and depth-first search</a>.
</p>

